ruct three layer-specific networks matching each agent’s degree targets (family, work/school, community); total degree emerges from multiplex union.\n    - Apply optional homophily/mixing rules using age_group and occupation to wire edges plausibly (e.g., students with students in school, workers with workers in work).\n  - Interaction dynamics:\n    - Social influence on mask adoption should be computed per layer, allowing different influence strengths (e.g., family > work/school > community) and aggregated to update behavior.\n    - Age_group and occupation can modulate influence weights or contact frequency; risk_perception modulates susceptibility to peer influence.\n  - Data checks/notes:\n    - Validate total_connections consistency; handle any anomalies (e.g., young ages with work connections) per modeling assumptions rather than strict real-world constraints.\n\nFile: social_network.json (type: json)\n- Overall data structure and type\n  - JSON object mapping agent IDs (as strings) to adjacency lists (arrays of integers).\n  - Each agent record contains four arrays: family, work_school, community, and all.\n  - Topology is static (no timestamps); IDs appear to range 0–999.\n\n- Relationships or nested elements\n  - family, work_school, community represent distinct network layers (tie contexts).\n  - all is the deduplicated union of contacts across the three layers for that agent.\n  - Edges are listed from the focal agent to others; reciprocity is not guaranteed (i.e., directed adjacency lists). Convert to undirected if needed by symmetrizing.\n  - Layer sizes suggest stronger, smaller family networks; moderate work/school; larger community ties.\n\n- How this data should inform simulation entities or interactions\n  - Agents: each top-level key is an agent; initialize per-agent state (e.g., mask_wearing ∈ {0,1}, adoption threshold, susceptibility).\n  - Network layers: model as a multiplex graph; assign interaction frequency/weight and influence strength per layer (e.g., family > work_school > community).\n  - Contact process: per timestep, sample interactions from each layer according to layer-specific rates; or use all for unlayered simulations.\n  - Influence/adoption: compute likelihood of adopting/maintaining mask-wearing from neighbors’ states, weighted by layer; support directed influence unless symmetrized.\n  - Seeding and interventions: select initial adopters; target layers (e.g., workplace campaigns) by adjusting weights or probabilities within work_school edges.\n  - Metrics: use all to compute overall degree/centrality; use layer-specific degrees for targeted strategies.\n  - Data hygiene: verify that neighbors exist in the node set; ensure all equals union(family, work_school, community); handle potential asymmetry if undirected behavior is desired.\n\nFile: train_data.csv (type: csv)\nSemantic metadata summary for train_data.csv\n\n- Overall data structure and type:\n  - Rectangular CSV; long-format panel (time-series) data.\n  - Columns:\n    - day: integer, discrete daily time step.\n    - agent_id: integer, agent identifier.\n    - wearing_mask: boolean, agent’s behavioral state at that day.\n    - received_info: boolean, whether the agent received mask-related information that day.\n  - Composite key: (agent_id, day). No missing nested structures.\n\n- Relationships or nested elements:\n  - Temporal dependency within each agent_id across day (state transitions over time).\n  - wearing_mask is the outcome state; received_info is a potential driver/exposure.\n  - No explicit network topology or interactions between agents included; any contagion/peer effects are implicit and must be modeled or inferred externally.\n  - Enables derivation of transition events (e.g., adopt, cease) via lagged states per agent.\n\n- How this data should inform simulation entities or interactions:\n  - Entities: One agent per agent_id with state variables:\n    - mask_state ∈ {true, false}\n    - info_state ∈ {true, false