- day: integer, discrete daily time step.\\n    - agent_id: integer, agent identifier.\\n    - wearing_mask: boolean, agent\\u2019s behavioral state at that day.\\n    - received_info: boolean, whether the agent received mask-related information that day.\\n  - Composite key: (agent_id, day). No missing nested structures.\\n\\n- Relationships or nested elements:\\n  - Temporal dependency within each agent_id across day (state transitions over time).\\n  - wearing_mask is the outcome state; received_info is a potential driver/exposure.\\n  - No explicit network topology or interactions between agents included; any contagion/peer effects are implicit and must be modeled or inferred externally.\\n  - Enables derivation of transition events (e.g., adopt, cease) via lagged states per agent.\\n\\n- How this data should inform simulation entities or interactions:\\n  - Entities: One agent per agent_id with state variables:\\n    - mask_state \\u2208 {true, false}\\n    - info_state \\u2208 {true, false} (possibly transient/per-day)\\n  - Time step: daily scheduler aligned with day.\\n  - Calibration targets:\\n    - Baseline transition probabilities P(mask_t | mask_{t-1}) to capture inertia/relapse.\\n    - Incremental effect of information P(mask_t | mask_{t-1}, received_info_t or received_info_{t-1}).\\n    - Durations of adherence and rates of switching absent info (spontaneous adoption/cessation).\\n  - Interactions:\\n    - Use received_info events as exogenous inputs or as outcomes of network diffusion; calibrate info-spread parameters (e.g., per-edge transmission) so simulated received_info rates match observed frequencies.\\n    - Initialize agents\\u2019 day 0 states from data when available; validate simulated trajectories against per-agent adoption curves and aggregate prevalence over time.\\n  - Missing elements to be supplied by the simulation:\\n    - Network topology (e.g., ER/WS/BA) and peer influence rules.\\n    - Any heterogeneity beyond agent_id (no demographics provided)."\n    }\n  },\n  "enhancement_error": "\'NoneType\' object has no attribute \'get\'"\n}\n\nData Analysis (if available):\n{\n  "data_summary": {\n    "key_patterns": [\n      {\n        "name": "Multiplex network with heterogeneous degrees",\n        "description": "Family layers are small and dense, work/school are moderate, community layers are larger and more diffuse; overall degrees are right-skewed.",\n        "relevance": "Layer-specific structure determines contact opportunities and the strength of social influence on mask adoption."\n      },\n      {\n        "name": "Directed edges with partial reciprocity",\n        "description": "Adjacency lists are directed; not all ties are mutual. Reciprocity varies by layer (family highest, community lowest).",\n        "relevance": "Influence can be asymmetric; decision to symmetrize affects effective degree and cascade potential."\n      },\n      {\n        "name": "Age/occupation mixing",\n        "description": "Students primarily connect in the school/work layer; workers connect in the work layer; family spans ages; community shows mild homophily by age_group and occupation.",\n        "relevance": "Mixing patterns drive who influences whom; layer-specific mixing constraints should guide edge construction or filtering."\n      },\n      {\n        "name": "Initial mask-wearing heterogeneity",\n        "description": "Initial adopters are unevenly distributed across agents; prevalence varies by age_group and occupation.",\n        "relevance": "Starting conditions affect early diffusion trajectories; seeding should mirror observed heterogeneity."\n      },\n      {\n        "name": "Risk perception variability",\n        "description": "risk_perception spans 0\\u20131 and is likely unimodal or mildly right-skewed; suitable for Beta distribution fitting.",\n        "relevance": "Maps to susceptibility thresholds; higher risk perception generally increases adoption propensity."\n      },\n      {\n        "name": "Temporal inertia in mask-wearing",\n        "description": "Strong state persistence: P(mask_t=1 | mask_{t-1}=1) >> P(mask_t=1 | mask_{t-1}=0).",\n        "relevance": "Behavior exhibits momentum; calibration must include persistence parameters (on- and off-durations)."\n      },\n      {\n        "name": "Information exposure effect",\n        "description": "received_info on day t (or t-1) increases adoption probability among previously unmasked agents.",\n        "relevance": "Information acts as an exogenous driver and/or output of a diffusion process; calibrate its incremental effect separately from peer influence."\n      },\n      {\n        "name": "Degree\\u2013adoption linkage",\n        "description": "Agents with higher total degree show different adoption hazards (often higher adoption due to more exposure, but can vary by layer weights).",\n        "relevance": "Centrality heterogeneity can accelerate or dampen diffusion; important for targeting and scenario testing."\n      }\n    ],\n    "key_distributions": [\n      {\n        "name": "Layer-specific degree distributions",\n        "description": "Empirical distributions of degrees in family, work_school, and community layers computed from social_network.json (or degree targets from agent_attributes.csv).",\n        "parameters": "For each layer L: mean_deg_L, sd_deg_L, p50_deg_L, p90_deg_L, max_deg_L; optionally fit a Negative Binomial with (r_L, p_L)."\n      },\n      {\n        "name": "Total degree distribution",\n        "description": "Distribution of deduplicated all-layer degrees per agent (social_network.json[\'all\']).",\n        "parameters": "mean_deg_all, sd_deg_all, tail index (Pareto alpha if heavy tail), Gini coefficient for degree inequality."\n      },\n      {\n        "name": "Risk perception",\n        "description": "Distribution of risk_perception \\u2208 [0,1] across agents (agent_attributes.csv). Fit a Beta distribution.",\n        "parameters": "Beta(a, b) fit via method-of-moments or MLE; also report mean_risk and sd_risk."\n      },\n      {\n        "name": "Initial mask prevalence",\n        "description": "Share of agents initially wearing a mask (agent_attributes.initial_mask_wearing or train_data day 0).",\n        "parameters": "p_init_mask = mean(initial_mask_wearing)."\n      },\n      {\n        "name": "Information arrival rate",\n        "description": "Daily probability an agent receives info (train_data.received_info). May be overdispersed across agents.",\n        "parameters": "lambda_info = mean(received_info); across-agent variability: var_info_rate; optionally fit Beta-Binomial (alpha_info, beta_info)."\n      },\n      {\n        "name": "State transition probabilities",\n        "description": "Daily transition probabilities between masked and unmasked states (train_data).",\n        "parameters": "p_on_noinfo = P(Mt=1|Mt-1=0, info_t=0); p_on_info = P(Mt=1|Mt-1=0, info_t=1); p_off = P(Mt=0|Mt-1=1); duration_on ~ Geometric(p_off); duration_off ~ Geometric(p_on_noinfo)."\n      },\n      {\n        "name": "Reciprocity and clustering",\n        "description": "Per-layer reciprocity and clustering from social_network.json after symmetrization choice.",\n        "parameters": "recip_L = mutual_edges_L / directed_edges_L; clustering_L = global_clustering_coefficient(L)."\n      }\n    ],\n    "key_relationships": [\n      {\n        "variables": [\n          "risk_perception",\n          "initial_mask_wearing"\n        ],\n        "relationship": "Higher risk_perception associated with higher probability of initial_mask_wearing.",\n        "strength": "Quantify via logistic regression: logit(P(mask=1)) = \\u03b20 + \\u03b21*risk_perception (+ controls). Expect \\u03b21 > 0 (medium to strong)."\n      },\n      {\n        "variables": [\n          "received_info_t",\n          "mask_t (conditional on mask_{t-1}=0)"\n        ],\n        "relationship": "Information on day t increases adoption on day t.",\n        "strength": "Incremental odds ratio OR_info = exp(\\u03b2_info) > 1; estimate from train_data (medium to strong)."\n      },\n      {\n        "variables": [\n          "mask_{t-1}",\n          "mask_t"\n        ],\n        "relationship": "State persistence (inertia).",\n        "strength": "High; estimate p_off and p_on_noinfo from transition counts."\n      },\n      {\n        "variables": [\n          "neighbor_mask_prevalence (by layer)",\n          "adoption hazard"\n        ],\n        "relationship": "Higher fraction of masked neighbors increases adoption hazard, strongest in family layer.",\n        "strength": "Estimate via multi-layer weighted exposure model; expect family > work_school > community (medium)."\n      },\n      {\n        "variables": [\n          "degree_all",\n          "adoption probability"\n        ],\n        "relationship": "Adoption probability correlates with total degree due to increased exposures; nonlinear at high degrees.",\n        "strength": "Low to medium; verify via partial dependence controlling for risk_perception."\n      },\n      {\n        "variables": [\n          "age_group/occupation",\n          "layer degree composition"\n        ],\n        "relationship": "Students have higher work_school degrees; retirees have lower work_school degrees, higher community/family.",\n        "strength": "High; use to constrain network wiring or validate social_network.json."\n      },\n      {\n        "variables": [\n          "reciprocity",\n          "adoption clustering"\n        ],\n        "relationship": "Higher reciprocity relates to stronger local clustering of behavior (Moran\\u2019s I).",\n        "strength": "Medium; diagnostic for whether to symmetrize edges."\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  "simulation_parameters": {\n    "population_and_network": {\n      "num_agents": {\n        "value": "N = |unique(agent_id)| (from agent_attributes.csv or keys(social_network.json))",\n        "source": "agent_attributes.csv, social_network.json",\n        "confidence": "High",\n        "notes": "Ensure alignment of agent_id domains across files; drop or add isolates as needed."\n      },\n      "degree_sequences_by_layer": {\n        "value": "Empirical degree sequences per layer from social_network.json (post choice: directed vs symmetrized)",\n        "source": "social_network.json",\n        "confidence": "High",\n        "notes": "If constructing networks from targets (agent_attributes.csv), match target degrees via configuration model per layer with mixing constraints."\n      },\n      "reciprocity_by_layer": {\n        "value": "recip_family, recip_work_school, recip_community (compute from directed lists)",\n        "source": "social_network.json",\n        "confidence": "High",\n        "notes": "Use to decide whether to treat influence as directed; if symmetrizing, report pre- and post-values."\n      },\n      "clustering_by_layer": {\n        "value": "global_clustering_family, global_clustering_work_school, global_clustering_community",\n        "source": "social_network.json",\n        "confidence": "Medium",\n        "notes": "Compute on undirected version; match approximately in synthetic rewiring."\n      },\n      "mixing_matrices": {\n        "value": "P(contact | age_group_i \\u2192 age_group_j) and P(contact | occupation_i \\u2192 occupation_j) per layer",\n        "source": "social_network.json joined with agent_attributes.csv",\n        "confidence": "Medium",\n        "notes": "Estimate empirical mixing; enforce during edge wiring in synthetic networks."\n      },\n      "layer_influence_weights": {\n        "value": {\n          "family": 1.0,\n          "work_school": 0.6,\n          "community": 0.3\n        },\n        "source": "Expert prior; to be calibrated against train_data adoption dynamics",\n        "confidence": "Low",\n        "notes": "Relative weights in aggregating neighbor influence; calibrate to match adoption responsiveness across layers if layer exposures can be inferred."\n      },\n      "interaction_rates_per_layer_per_day": {\n        "value": {\n          "family": 0.8,\n          "work_school": 1.5,\n          "community": 1.0\n        },\n        "source": "Expert prior; adjust to match contact frequency assumptions",\n        "confidence": "Low",\n        "notes": "Expected number of salient interactions per agent per day by layer; scales the amount of social exposure."\n      }\n    },\n    "behavior_dynamics": {\n      "initial_mask_prevalence": {\n        "value": "p_init_mask = mean(agent_attributes.initial_mask_wearing) or prevalence at day=min(train_data.day)",\n        "source": "agent_attributes.csv and/or train_data.csv",\n        "confidence": "High",\n        "notes": "Use agent_attributes for seeding unless time series provides an earlier baseline."\n      },\n      "p_adopt_no_info": {\n        "value": "P(Mt=1 | Mt-1=0, received_info_t=0)",\n        "source": "train_data.csv",\n        "confidence": "High",\n        "notes": "Compute from transitions; serves as baseline adoption absent info and peer effects."\n      },\n      "p_adopt_with_info": {\n        "value": "P(Mt=1 | Mt-1=0, received_info_t=1)",\n        "source": "train_data.csv",\n        "confidence": "High",\n        "notes": "Captures incremental effect of info on same day (can also include received_info_{t-1})."\n      },\n      "p_drop": {\n        "value": "P(Mt=0 | Mt-1=1)",\n        "source": "train_data.csv",\n        "confidence": "High",\n        "notes": "Controls adherence decay; can be modeled as constant or dependent on info/peers."\n      },\n      "peer_influence_coefficient": {\n        "value": "\\u03b2_peer (log-odds increase per unit weighted neighbor_mask_share)",\n        "source": "Calibrate using train_data with simulated exposure or using social_network.json + observed neighbor states if available",\n        "confidence": "Medium",\n        "notes": "Use logistic model: logit(P(adopt)) = \\u03b20 + \\u03b2_info*info + \\u03b2_peer*E_t + \\u03b2_risk*risk + interactions; E_t = \\u03a3_L w_L * masked_share_L."\n      },\n      "risk_susceptibility_scaler": {\n        "value": 1.0,\n        "source": "Expert prior; refine to fit",\n        "confidence": "Low",\n        "notes": "Maps risk_perception into threshold/susceptibility: \\u03b8_i = \\u03b80 - \\u03b1*risk_i; start \\u03b1=1.0."\n      },\n      "exogenous_noise_flip": {\n        "value": 0.005,\n        "source": "Expert prior",\n        "confidence": "Low",\n        "notes": "Small probability of random state change to account for unobserved shocks."\n      }\n    },\n    "information_dynamics": {\n      "external_info_rate": {\n        "value": "lambda_info = mean(received_info_t)",\n        "source": "train_data.csv",\n        "confidence": "High",\n        "notes": "If modeling info as diffusion, treat this as baseline exogenous rate."\n      },\n      "peer_info_transmission_prob_per_contact": {\n        "value": {\n          "family": "\\u03c4_family",\n          "work_school": "\\u03c4_work_school",\n          "community": "\\u03c4_community"\n        },\n        "source": "Calibrate so simulated received_info prevalence and clustering match train_data",\n        "confidence": "Medium",\n        "notes": "Use multiplex independent cascade for info spread; constrain \\u03c4_family \\u2265 \\u03c4_work_school \\u2265 \\u03c4_community."\n      },\n      "info_effect_size_on_adoption": {\n        "value": "\\u03b2_info = log(OR_info) where OR_info = p_on_info*(1-p_on_noinfo) / [(1-p_on_info)*p_on_noinfo]",\n        "source": "Derived from train_data.csv transitions",\n        "confidence": "High",\n        "notes": "Plug into adoption logistic model; can allow lagged effects \\u03b2_info_lag."\n      },\n      "info_memory_decay_days": {\n        "value": 1,\n        "source": "Assumption based on train_data definition (daily flag)",\n        "confidence": "Medium",\n        "notes": "If evidence of lagged effects, extend to 2\\u20133 days with decay."\n      }\n    },\n    "scheduler_and_timing": {\n      "time_step_days": {\n        "value": 1,\n        "source": "train_data.csv (daily time index)",\n        "confidence": "High",\n        "notes": "Synchronous daily updates."\n      },\n      "warmup_days": {\n        "value": 0,\n        "source": "Design choice",\n        "confidence": "Low",\n        "notes": "Set >0 if initializing from agent_attributes but validating against later train_data window."\n      },\n      "simulation_horizon_days": {\n        "value": "match max(train_data.day) - min(train_data.day) + 1",\n        "source": "train_data.csv",\n        "confidence": "High",\n        "notes": "Extend for forecasting scenarios."\n      }\n    },\n    "observation_and_validation": {\n      "observed_variables": {\n        "value": [\n          "wearing_mask (state)",\n          "received_info (event)"\n        ],\n        "source": "train_data.csv",\n        "confidence": "High",\n        "notes": "Assumed measured without error; if not, add observation noise."\n      },\n      "target_moments": {\n        "value": [\n          "daily prevalence",\n          "transition rates",\n          "duration distributions",\n          "agent-level adoption curves"\n        ],\n        "source": "train_data.csv",\n        "confidence": "High",\n        "notes": "Use as calibration and validation targets."\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  "calibration_strategy": {\n    "preprocessing_steps": [\n      {\n        "step": "Align agent_id across files and restrict to intersection set",\n        "purpose": "Ensure consistent population across attributes, network, and time series."\n      },\n      {\n        "step": "Validate total_connections consistency",\n        "purpose": "Check agent_attributes total_connections equals sum of layer-specific degrees; flag anomalies."\n      },\n      {\n        "step": "Network sanitation and symmetrization decision",\n        "purpose": "Compute reciprocity; choose directed influence or symmetrize adjacency; construct undirected edges if required."\n      },\n      {\n        "step": "Compute per-layer degrees, clustering, and mixing matrices",\n        "purpose": "Summarize topology for configuration and synthetic network generation if needed."\n      },\n      {\n        "step": "Construct per-agent daily lag features",\n        "purpose": "From train_data, create mask_{t-1}, received_info_t, received_info_{t-1}, and transition indicators."\n      },\n      {\n        "step": "Estimate baseline transition probabilities",\n        "purpose": "Compute p_on_noinfo, p_on_info, p_off and duration distributions as initial calibration targets."\n      },\n      {\n        "step": "Join risk_perception and demographics to time series",\n        "purpose": "Enable heterogeneous effect estimation and stratified validation."\n      }\n    ],\n    "calibration_approach": "Use a staged (hybrid frequentist + simulation-based) approach: (1) Estimate direct-from-data parameters: initial_mask_prevalence, p_on_noinfo, p_on_info, p_off, lambda_info, Beta(a,b) for risk_perception, degree/mixing summaries. (2) Fit a logistic model on train_data: logit(P(Mt=1)) = \\u03b20 + \\u03b3*Mt-1 + \\u03b2_info*received_info_t + \\u03b2_risk*risk_i + \\u03b2_demo*X_i. This yields \\u03b2_info, persistence \\u03b3, and baseline. (3) In the agent-based simulation, incorporate network exposure E_i,t = \\u03a3_L w_L * masked_share among neighbors per layer and add \\u03b2_peer for peer effects. Calibrate \\u03b2_peer, layer weights w_L, and peer info transmission \\u03c4_L via simulation-based optimization (e.g., ABC-SMC or Bayesian optimization) to match observed prevalence trajectory, transition rates, and (if available) spatial/network clustering of adoption. (4) If modeling info diffusion endogenously, match simulated received_info rates and their temporal autocorrelation to train_data. Prioritize identifiability by fixing either \\u03b2_peer or \\u03c4_L initially, then release both.",\n    "validation_strategy": "Hold out a time window (e.g., last 20% of days) and validate: (a) Aggregate prevalence: RMSE/MAE between simulated and observed daily mask prevalence. (b) Transition rates: compare p_on and p_off by day and by strata (age_group, occupation, degree quintiles). (c) Micro-level trajectories: Hamming distance or dynamic Brier score for agent-level mask states. (d) Network patterns: if neighbor states over time are available or reconstructable, validate clustering (Moran\\u2019s I) and community-level prevalence. Perform k-fold time-slice cross-validation and sensitivity analysis over key parameters (\\u03b2_peer, w_L, \\u03c4_L).",\n    "key_variables_to_calibrate": [\n      "\\u03b2_peer (peer influence strength)",\n      "w_L per layer (influence weights family/work_school/community)",\n      "\\u03c4_L per layer (info transmission probabilities)",\n      "\\u03b1 (risk_susceptibility_scaler)",\n      "interaction_rates_per_layer",\n      "exogenous_noise_flip"\n    ]\n  },\n  "file_summaries": [\n    {\n      "file_name": "agent_attributes.csv",\n      "file_type": "csv",\n      "semantic_summary": "- Overall data structure and type:\\n  - Flat CSV table; one row per agent.\\n  - Primary key: agent_id (integer, unique).\\n  - Columns/types:\\n    - age (int), age_group (categorical string), occupation (categorical string)\\n    - risk_perception (float 0\\u20131)\\n    - initial_mask_wearing (boolean)\\n    - family_connections, work_school_connections, community_connections, total_connections (integers)\\n  - total_connections equals the sum of the three layer-specific connection counts.\\n\\n- Relationships or nested elements:\\n  - No nested records or explicit edge lists; relationships are implicit via degree counts per social layer.\\n  - Implies a multiplex network with three layers: family, work/school, community. Each agent has a target degree in each layer.\\n  - Occupation and age_group suggest mixing constraints within layers (e.g., students in school layer; workers in work layer), but this is not explicitly encoded\\u2014must be inferred during network construction.\\n\\n- How this data should inform simulation entities or interactions:\\n  - Agent initialization:\\n    - Create one agent per row with attributes: age/age_group, occupation, risk_perception, initial_mask_wearing.\\n    - initial_mask_wearing sets the agent\\u2019s starting behavior state.\\n    - risk_perception provides a baseline propensity or threshold for adopting mask-wearing.\\n  - Network generation:\\n    - Construct three layer-specific networks matching each agent\\u2019s degree targets (family, work/school, community); total degree emerges from multiplex union.\\n    - Apply optional homophily/mixing rules using age_group and occupation to wire edges plausibly (e.g., students with students in school, workers with workers in work).\\n  - Interaction dynamics:\\n    - Social influence on mask adoption should be computed per layer, allowing different influence strengths (e.g., family > work/school > community) and aggregated to update behavior.\\n    - Age_group and occupation can modulate influence weights or contact frequency; risk_perception modulates susceptibility to peer influence.\\n  - Data checks/notes:\\n    - Validate total_connections consistency; handle any anomalies (e.g., young ages with work connections) per modeling assumptions rather than strict real-world constraints."\n    },\n    {\n      "file_name": "social_network.json",\n      "file_type": "json",\n      "semantic_summary": "- Overall data structure and type\\n  - JSON object mapping agent IDs (as strings) to adjacency lists (arrays of integers).\\n  - Each agent record contains four arrays: family, work_school, community, and all.\\n  - Topology is static (no timestamps); IDs appear to range 0\\u2013999.\\n\\n- Relationships or nested elements\\n  - family, work_school, community represent distinct network layers (tie contexts).\\n  - all is the deduplicated union of contacts across the three layers for that agent.\\n  - Edges are listed from the focal agent to others; reciprocity is not guaranteed (i.e., directed adjacency lists). Convert to undirected if needed by symmetrizing.\\n  - Layer sizes suggest stronger, smaller family networks; moderate work/school; larger community ties.\\n\\n- How this data should inform simulation entities or interactions\\n  - Agents: each top-level key is an agent; initialize per-agent state (e.g., mask_wearing \\u2208 {0,1}, adoption threshold, susceptibility).\\n  - Network layers: model as a multiplex graph; assign interaction frequency/weight and influence strength per layer (e.g., family > work_school > community).\\n  - Contact process: per timestep, sample interactions from each layer according to layer-specific rates; or use all for unlayered simulations.\\n  - Influence/adoption: compute likelihood of adopting/maintaining mask-wearing from neighbors\\u2019 states, weighted by layer; support directed influence unless symmetrized.\\n  - Seeding and interventions: select initial adopters; target layers (e.g., workplace campaigns) by adjusting weights or probabilities within work_school edges.\\n  - Metrics: use all to compute overall degree/centrality; use layer-specific degrees for targeted strategies.\\n  - Data hygiene: verify that neighbors exist in the node set; ensure all equals union(family, work_school, community); handle potential asymmetry if undirected behavior is desired."\n    },\n    {\n      "file_name": "train_data.csv",\n      "file_type": "csv",\n      "semantic_summary": "Semantic metadata summary for train_data.csv\\n\\n- Overall data structure and type:\\n  - Rectangular CSV; long-format panel (time-series) data.\\n  - Columns:\\n    - day: integer, discrete daily time step.\\n    - agent_id: integer, agent identifier.\\n    - wearing_mask: boolean, agent\\u2019s behavioral state at that day.\\n    - received_info: boolean, whether the agent received mask-related information that day.\\n  - Composite key: (agent_id, day). No missing nested structures.\\n\\n- Relationships or nested elements:\\n  - Temporal dependency within each agent_id across day (state transitions over time).\\n  - wearing_mask is the outcome state; received_info is a potential driver/exposure.\\n  - No explicit network topology or interactions between agents included; any contagion/peer effects are implicit and must be modeled or inferred externally.\\n  - Enables derivation of transition events (e.g., adopt, cease) via lagged states per agent.\\n\\n- How this data should inform simulation entities or interactions:\\n  - Entities: One agent per agent_id with state variables:\\n    - mask_state \\u2208 {true, false}\\n    - info_state \\u2208 {true, false} (possibly transient/per-day)\\n  - Time step: daily scheduler aligned with day.\\n  - Calibration targets:\\n    - Baseline transition probabilities P(mask_t | mask_{t-1}) to capture inertia/relapse.\\n    - Incremental effect of information P(mask_t | mask_{t-1}, received_info_t or received_info_{t-1}).\\n    - Durations of adherence and rates of switching absent info (spontaneous adoption/cessation).\\n  - Interactions:\\n    - Use received_info events as exogenous inputs or as outcomes of network diffusion; calibrate info-spread parameters (e.g., per-edge transmission) so simulated received_info rates match observed frequencies.\\n    - Initialize agents\\u2019 day 0 states from data when available; validate simulated trajectories against per-agent adoption curves and aggregate prevalence over time.\\n  - Missing elements to be supplied by the simulation:\\n    - Network topology (e.g., ER/WS/BA) and peer influence rules.\\n    - Any heterogeneity beyond agent_id (no demographics provided)."\n    }\n  ]\n}\n\nPlease design a simulation model that addresses the task requirements. Your response should include:\n\n1. The most appropriate modeling approach (agent-based, system dynamics, network-based, etc.)\n2. The structure of the simulation (entities, behaviors, interactions)\n3. The environment representation (grid, continuous space, network, etc.)\n4. Necessary parameters and configuration options\n5. Initialization procedures\n6. The algorithms to implement key behaviors and interactions\n7. A plan for how the code should be structured\n8. Any prediction or evaluation period specified by the task (e.g., days 30-39), and include it as a `prediction_period` field in the model plan.\n9. The evaluation metrics required by the task, and include them as an `evaluation_metrics` list in the model plan.\n\nResponse Format:\nPlease structure your response as a JSON object with the following structure:\n{\n  "model_type": "agent_based | system_dynamics | network | etc.",\n  "description": "Brief description of the model approach",\n  "entities": [\n    {\n      "name": "Entity name",\n      "attributes": ["attribute1", "attribute2", ...],\n      "behaviors": ["behavior1", "behavior2", ...],\n      "initialization": {\n        "method": "random | data_driven | specified",\n        "parameters": { ... }\n      }\n    }\n  ],\n  "behaviors": [\n    {\n      "name": "behavior_name",\n      "description": "Description of the behavior",\n      "applicable_to": ["Entity1", "Entity2", ...],\n      "parameters": { ... },\n      "algorithm": "Description of algorithm or approach"\n    }\n  ],\n  "interactions": [\n    {\n      "name": "interaction_name",\n      "description": "Description of the interaction",\n      "entities_involved": ["Entity1", "Entity2", ...],\n      "trigger": "proximity | time | state | etc.",\n      "effect": "Description of the effect"\n    }\n  ],\n  "environment": {\n    "type": "grid | continuous | network | etc.",\n    "dimensions": [x, y] or null,\n    "time_step": value,\n    "time_unit": "seconds | minutes | hours | days | etc."\n  },\n  "parameters": {\n    "param1": value1,\n    "param2": value2,\n    ...\n  },\n  "initialization": {\n    "population_size": number,\n    "random_seed": number,\n    ...\n  },\n  "algorithms": {\n    "algorithm1": "Description or name of algorithm",\n    "algorithm2": "Description or name of algorithm",\n    ...\n  },\n  "data_sources": [\n    {\n      "name": "data_source_name",\n      "purpose": "Purpose of using this data",\n      "fields_used": ["field1", "field2", ...]\n    }\n  ],\n  "code_structure": {\n    "files": [\n      {\n        "name": "filename.py",\n        "description": "Purpose of this file"\n      }\n    ],\n    "dependencies": [\n      "library1",\n      "library2",\n      ...\n    ]\n  },\n  "prediction_period": { "start_day": <integer>, "end_day": <integer> },\n  "evaluation_metrics": [ "metric1", "metric2", ... ]\n} '}]}], 'max_output_tokens': 10000, 'model': 'gpt-5-2025-08-07'}}
